true-interaction recall:
93.20%.

false promotion:
24.30%.

total promoted:
39.45%.

mean net improvement:
0.2295
per candidate.

10th-percentile candidate outcome:
~-0.512.

1st percentile:
~-2.21.

PRIMARY PASSIVE-PROMOTION NEGATIVE
Passive residual thresholding
finds:
most true interactions.

But:
noise generates:
many false permanent grammar terms.

The lower tail is:
bad.

Representation growth
becomes:
multiple-comparison overfit.

VALIDATE EVERY NOMINEE

true recall:
92.19%.

false promotion:
1.10%.

total promoted:
21.13%.

mean net improvement:
0.2144.

PRIMARY TEST-EVERYTHING RESULT
Independent challenge
almost eliminates:
false grammar growth.

But:
challenging:
every passive residual
costs:
too much.

The policy improves:
model correctness

without:
maximizing:
net value.

VALUE-GATED CHALLENGE

challenge audition rate:
~16.0%
of all candidates.

final promoted:
~13.2%.

true-interaction recall:
~59.2%.

false promotion:
~0.16%.

mean net improvement:
0.2992
per candidate.

PRIMARY VALUE-GATED POSITIVE
The strongest tested policy
deliberately ignores:
many:
real but low-value
higher-order interactions.

It spends:
challenge budget
on:
the interactions
that matter.

This yields:
the highest net value
despite:
lower raw recall.

VALUE CAPTURE
Among:
all true higher-order interactions,

the promoted value-gated subset
contains:
approximately 86.4%
of:
total future semantic value.

Among:
the highest-value quartile
of true interactions,

promotion recall:
approximately 94.7%.

Thus:
the policy is:
selectively incomplete
in:
the useful direction.

HIGH-STAKES PRINCIPLE
A rare harmful interaction
can deserve:
challenge

even if:
its occurrence count
is low.

Candidate value depends on:

EFFECT SIZE

x

EXPECTED EXPOSURE

x

CONSEQUENCE STAKE.

Not:
frequency alone.

